  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Prolonged stretch of dry weather across southern New \nEngland. Moderating afternoon temperatures this weekend, though \nchilly mornings will bring widespread frost away from the immediate \ncoast.\n\nIf the forecast discussion starts sounding like a broken record over \nthe next several days, you can thank strong high pressure for that! \nAn extended stretch of dry weather is expected, with some day-to-day \nvariability in temperatures, but otherwise not much excitement on \nthe horizon. In fact, global ensembles paint a rather parched \npicture for southern New England, with cumulative probabilities of \nmeasurable precipitation (0.01 inches or greater) generally less \nthan 10 percent through much of next week.\n\nAn expansive area of surface high pressure, around 1035 mb, remains \nanchored across the region this weekend, with some guidance \nsuggesting pressures could approach 1040 mb Saturday night. This \nwill promote plenty of sunshine, light winds, and generally quiet \nconditions. Temperatures at 925 mb warm to +6C to +9C this \nafternoon, supporting highs in the upper 50s to lower 60s. If this \nwere a few weeks earlier, with a higher sun angle, we'd likely be \ntalking about highs in the middle to upper 60s. But here we are, \nnearing the end of October! Temperatures aloft continue to moderate \nSunday and Monday, with 925 mb temperatures approaching +10C Sunday \nand +13C Monday. This should support highs in the lower to middle \n60s Sunday, with some locations potentially reaching 70F Monday.\n\nOvernight temperatures, however, are a different story. Clear skies \nand light to calm winds beneath strong high pressure will promote \nefficient radiational cooling. Opted to trend overnight lows below \nNBM guidance, which typically runs too warm in these setups. \nSaturday and Sunday mornings, and to some extent Monday morning, \nwill be quite cold, with lows in the 20s to lower 30s and widespread \nfrost away from the immediate coast and urban heat islands. Be sure \nto protect those mums and Halloween pumpkins! One interesting \nfeature of this setup will be the potential for significant \ntemperature differences between sheltered valleys and higher \nelevations. With clear skies and light winds promoting strong \nradiational cooling, colder air will settle into the valleys, while \nsome elevated locations may remain several degrees warmer within the \nnocturnal temperature inversion.\n\nLooking ahead, a moisture-starved cold front approaches from the \nnorth-northwest late Monday night into early Tuesday. However, \npersistent high pressure and dry air will likely limit the front to \nlittle more than some additional cloud cover and a cooler air mass. \nHighs return to the 50s Tuesday and Wednesday before potentially \nrebounding into the 60s Thursday and Friday. Overnight temperatures \nshould trend closer to seasonable levels, although confidence is \nlower given uncertainty surrounding cloud cover and winds, both of \nwhich could limit radiational cooling. Fingers crossed this dry \npattern holds just a little longer and gives us a dry All Hallows' \nEve!",
